# Freeform creature construction

- Treat Roblox primitives as raw sculpting material, not finished anatomy.
- Never leave one obvious Ball, Block, Wedge, or Cylinder as a finished head, torso, muzzle, hand,
  or foot. Intersect, taper, scale, and rotate a few broad masses into one organic silhouette.
- Reserve spheres for small details such as eyes, buds, joints, and glints; do not stack large
  spheres into the main body plan.
- Hide limb attachment points and build hands and feet from palms or soles with tapered digits.
- A carved mouth must be recessed negative space framed by brow, cheek, and jaw masses. A dark
  plate on the surface is not a carved mouth.
- Check front, three-quarter, and side views at gameplay distance. If a major region reads first as
  a primitive shape, rework it before adding decoration.
- Stay within the approved primitive-only pipeline. Freeforming does not authorize MeshParts,
  unions, or imported geometry.
- Read `.agents/skills/organic-roblox-form/SKILL.md` before creature concept, preview, or review work.
